--- That blocker is now resolved (2026-09-01, later the same night) ---

The bot no longer depends on Helius, or on any paid plan or key. Two free
public endpoints were measured from a runner and now backstop every
configured one: a blockhash in about a tenth of a second, fifteen calls
with no refusal, and an accepted sendTransaction, which is what proves the
send path is open rather than throttled. Endpoints are tried in order, so
one being exhausted is a slower call rather than an unsellable position.

Execution was measured too, since it had only ever been assumed: quote,
build and sign together take 0.17 seconds, not the ten to twenty this plan
was designed around. With detection at about a second, entry lands far
inside the window where the measured edge is positive.

What has NOT been established, and what the pilot exists to find out:

  - that a transaction actually LANDS under real congestion. The probe
    proves an endpoint accepts a send; only spending real fees proves
    landing, which is why the pilot is $30 and not $300.
  - that real slippage is anywhere near the 3.2% round-trip the backtest
    assumes. Break-even is 12.6% against a +8.7% modelled edge, so if
    slippage runs worse than modelled the edge is gone. This is the single
    number the pilot must measure.

So this file stays until Carter says go. The reason it was written no
longer applies; the decision to spend real money is still his.
